"smart_sources:Clippings/Probability Theory Fundamentals for Quantitative Finance - Interactive.md": {"class_name":"SmartSource","last_read":{"hash":"1u4gwdc","at":1794036911236},"embedding":{"history":[],"default":{"mf_ccarbz":{"file":"mf_ccarbz","file_i":35,"read_hash":"1u4gwdc","at":1794036929174}}},"path":"Clippings/Probability Theory Fundamentals for Quantitative Finance - Interactive.md","last_import":{"mtime":1785652197575,"size":74285,"at":1794036911274,"hash":"1u4gwdc"},"outlinks":[{"title":"Michael Brenndoerfer","target":"Michael Brenndoerfer","line":5},{"title":"Probability theory","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":19},{"title":"standard deviation","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/descriptive-statistics-guide-python-data-analysis","line":21},{"title":"portfolio optimization","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/differential-calculus-optimization-quantitative-finance","line":21},{"title":"expected loss","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/credit-risk-fundamentals-pd-lgd-ead-expected-loss","line":23},{"title":"probability distributions","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-distributions-guide-data-science","line":23},{"title":"-1, \\infty)$, since returns cannot be less than -100% but can, in principle, be arbitrarily large (though extreme returns are rare). Notice how the nature of the random experiment determines the appropriate sample space: a finite set for the die, an infinite continuum for stock returns. Choosing the right sample space is the first modeling decision we make, and it shapes all subsequent analysis.\n\nEvent\n\nAn event $A$ is a subset of the sample space, $A \\subseteq \\Omega$. Events represent outcomes we care about, such as \"rolling an even number\" or \"the stock return exceeds 5%.\"\n\nEvents emerge from our questions about uncertain situations. While the sample space contains raw outcomes, events package those outcomes into meaningful categories that correspond to questions we want to answer. The event \"rolling an even number\" is the subset $\\{2, 4, 6\\}$, which collects all the individual outcomes where an even number appears. The event \"stock return exceeds 5%\" contains every possible return value greater than 0.05. By defining events as subsets, we gain access to the powerful machinery of set theory for combining and manipulating them.\n\nEvents can be combined using set operations. If $A$ is \"stock rises\" and $B$ is \"volume is high,\" then:\n\n- $A \\cup B$ is \"stock rises OR volume is high (or both)\"\n- $A \\cap B$ is \"stock rises AND volume is high\"\n- $A^c$ is \"stock does not rise\"\n\nThese set operations allow us to build complex events from simpler ones, mirroring how we naturally combine conditions in financial analysis. When a portfolio manager asks \"what's the probability that either the market rallies or volatility spikes?\" they're implicitly using the union operation. The intersection operation captures joint occurrences, while the complement captures the negation of an event.\n\n### The Kolmogorov Axioms\n\nWith sample spaces and events defined, we need rules for assigning probabilities to events. Andrey Kolmogorov formalized [probability theory","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":35},{"title":"Normalization","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/normalization-feature-scaling-min-max-machine-learning-guide","line":56},{"title":"probability theory","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":64},{"title":"random variables","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":75},{"title":"random variables","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":87},{"title":"random variables","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":93},{"title":"Bar chart showing probabilities for Upgraded (15%), Unchanged (70%), and Downgraded (15%) credit states.","target":"https://assets.mbrenndoerfer.com/notebooks/2_probability_fundamentals_files/pmf-credit-rating-transitions.png","line":128,"embedded":true},{"title":"random variables","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":136},{"title":"probability theory","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":144},{"title":"random variables","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":150},{"title":"probability distributions","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-distributions-guide-data-science","line":150},{"title":"random variables","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-basics-foundation-statistical-reasoning","line":167},{"title":"Line plot of the standard normal PDF with a vertical line at x = 1 and the area for x ≤ 1 shaded.","target":"https://assets.mbrenndoerfer.com/notebooks/2_probability_fundamentals_files/pdf-shaded-area-normal.png","line":175,"embedded":true},{"title":"Line plot of the standard normal CDF with vertical and horizontal reference lines marking F(1).","target":"https://assets.mbrenndoerfer.com/notebooks/2_probability_fundamentals_files/cdf-cumulative-normal.png","line":179,"embedded":true},{"title":"probability distributions","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/probability-distributions-guide-data-science","line":187},{"title":"Central Limit Theorem","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/central-limit-theorem-foundation-statistical-inference","line":191},{"title":"financial modeling","target":"https://mbrenndoerfer.com/writing/introduction-stock-market-monte-carlo-simulation","line":191},{"title":"random variables","target":"https:/